drills.explanation:
Compare the two contracts by name, once per direction, and collect sorted messages:

```java
for (var old : was.values()) {
    var f = now.get(old.name());
    if (f == null) { if (old.required()) out.add("removed: " + old.name()); }
    else if (f.type() != old.type()) out.add("type changed: " + old.name());
    else if (old.required() && !f.required()) out.add("may be missing: " + old.name());
}
if (readerFailsOnUnknown) for (var f : now.values()) if (!was.containsKey(f.name())) out.add("unknown: " + f.name());
```

`breaksOldWriters` is the mirror image: it walks `after` and asks what an old body lacks. The tests catch the plausible wrong rules: flagging every removed field instead of only required ones (the optional `note` removal reports nothing), treating a rename as one harmless change (the rename test wants a removal), checking only the reader direction (the new required `currency` must break writers), and flagging a field that merely became required in a response (the reverse case expects an empty list).

